Grouping into words alone was not enough: WhisperX stretched け (0.74 s) and ど (0.48 s) over the pause, so even the whole word けど overlapped the guest's diarization turn more than the host's. Bunsetsu-level phrases (content word + following particles/auxiliaries, e.g. お聞きしてるんですけど) average the overlap over a span long enough to outweigh the misaligned characters, and nobody changes speaker inside one. Snapping diarization turn boundaries to the nearest word boundary was considered and rejected: on this data both edges of the short guest turn snapped to the same boundary and the turn vanished arbitrarily. The weak-support rule targets runs that only tip to a speaker by a sliver of overlap; well-supported short replies are kept. Groupers are per-language modules returning (surface, starts_phrase) so the char->phrase mapping is written once; Chinese uses rjieba (already installed with f5-tts) plus a particle list because rjieba.cut has no POS tags.
